		<div class="post" id="post-3776298">
		<div class="long">
			<div class="head">
									<div class="options">
						<a href="javascript:;" onclick="togglePostFold(event,3776298)" class="btn btn-default btn-small btn-sm">Fold</a>
					</div>
								<div class="title" id="post-title-3776298">
																		
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/the-great-hippo" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(3938622); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=3938622&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606014675" alt="The Great Hippo" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=3938622)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/the-great-hippo" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(3938622); return false;" >The Great Hippo</a></span> <span class="odate time_1524318044 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">21 Apr 2018 13:40</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-3776298">
									

<p>I don't want to lead you astray; my style of writing is deeply improvisational, which makes it hard for me to nail down precisely why what I do works when it does. That being said, sure &#8212; but take everything I'm saying here with <em>prescription grade</em> salt:</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Tips?&nbsp;I&nbsp;guess?</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<ul>
<li>For something like this, you don't <em>actually</em> need to know what's going on. But you do have to have a vague idea of what's going on. For example, I know the Cotter family had a secret; it had something to do with one of their children and their basement. I know they plugged their drains and faucets up because something was rising up through the pipes from below. I know that's also why they hid in the attic. I also know that's why the Bonafedes hid in the trees.</li>
<li>Once you have your 'vague idea', the trick is to get the reader to understand what's going on without ever telling them what's going on. If you just tell the reader: &quot;<em>There's a monster in the basement, and it's bleeding out into the soil! It's coming up through the pipes, the concrete, even through the goddamn <strong>water</strong>!</em>&quot; &#8212; that's kind of scary, sure&#8230; but not half as scary if you put the reader in a place where they come to realize that without ever being told.</li>
<li>The moment the reader realizes that something is there <em>just</em> to be spooky, the jig is up. That's why the above tactic works so well: Once the reader gets a sense that &quot;something is going on here&quot;, they'll start connecting those dots for you &#8212; they'll even connect dots you never intended them to connect! The flip-side of this: Don't include elements unless you have at least a <em>vague</em> idea of why they're there. Example: Why do animals refuse to enter SCP-3219? Answer: &quot;They can sense that the earth there has gone sour.&quot; Why does this place cause people to develop a fear of being buried alive? Because basements are scary places that are buried underground. There doesn't have to be a concrete, clear answer, but there has to be a thematic/narrative thread. It has to make sense <em>emotionally</em>, if not <em>logically</em>.</li>
<li>To put this another way: You're more or less engaging in a game of writing prompts with the reader. Your job, then, is to make sure those writing prompts are 1) Compelling, 2) Thematically consistent, and 3) Part of a steady escalation. This way, you kind of end up collaborating with the reader to help them create their own story to fill in the gaps you left.</li>
<li>Monsters are scary, but the absence of a monster can be even scarier. In the (paraphrased) words of Stephen King: No matter how scary you make your 7-foot tall cockroach, readers will always think &quot;Scary, but at least it wasn't an <em>8-foot</em> tall cockroach!&quot;. Not showing the monster is how you leave room for the reader to terrify themselves.</li>
<li>In the (again, paraphrased) words of <span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/soullesssingularity" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(637830); return false;"><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=637830&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1524318508" alt="SoullessSingularity" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=637830)" /></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/soullesssingularity" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(637830); return false;">SoullessSingularity</a></span>: Death isn't scary. Death is a release. Death is when the horror <em>ends</em>. But not <em>knowing</em> what happened? That's way, way scarier.</li>
<li><strong>ETA:</strong> Something else that's terrifying (which is something that I relied on heavily for this piece) &#8212; behavior that makes no sense to us, but leaves us with the sense that it made perfect sense to the people who did it. The act of plugging your faucets and drains up with plaster &#8212; hiding in your attic &#8212; hiding in the trees. Why would people do that? We don't know, but if we believe they had good reasons, we'll find ourselves afraid of finding out what those good reasons were.</li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>I kind of wrote this all off the cuff, because I'm on my way out the door. Hopefully some of it helps? Either way, good luck!</p>

